  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through Monday)\nIssued at 1139 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe weak front should move through the entire area by Wednesday\nmorning, with a noticeable decrease in low level moisture, north\nto northeast winds and a modest cooldown. High temperatures on\nWednesday should still be near to above normal in most spots but\nshould feel nicer given the lower humidity and certainly compared\nto expectations on Tuesday. The influence of the front will linger\ninto Thursday, but southerly winds and a return to heat and\nhumidity is expected Friday. Highs on Friday have been adjusted\ndownward a couple of degrees from the NBM initialization using the\nblended traditional MOS guidance given the aforementioned\noverforecasting by the NBM in similar scenarios. An expected\nincrease in cloud cover should also serve as a limiting factor\nregarding how high temperatures could be. A low chance of shower \nand thunderstorms across parts of northeast Oklahoma into \nnorthwest Arkansas will develop Thursday night into Friday in \nresponse to disturbances moving southeast along the edge of the \nupper high. This potential should expand southward Friday night\ninto Saturday, especially across far eastern Oklahoma and western\nArkansas. The increased cloud cover and precipitation on Saturday\nboth make the high temperature forecast for Saturday somewhat\nuncertain, with a decent gradient in temperatures from west to\neast likely across the area. The upper level high should remain \nstretched across the region Sunday and into early next week,\nleading to an additional stretch of well above normal temperatures\nand extreme heat. Confidence is medium to high that the NBM high\ntemperatures are too high during this period, but a day or two \nwith triple digit highs and certainly additional heat headlines \nshould be expected.",
